WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:11.784
In the last video, the warehouse floor had fixed parts: new-arrivals shelves, a short-stay shelf, and one big room of long-term storage.

2
00:00:11.784 --> 00:00:14.779
G1, the default collector, changes one thing.

3
00:00:15.667 --> 00:00:19.329
But first, a question that's easy to be shy about.

4
00:00:19.329 --> 00:00:22.990
G1 aims to keep each pause under 200 milliseconds.

5
00:00:22.990 --> 00:00:24.968
A heap can be 40 gigabytes.

6
00:00:24.968 --> 00:00:26.872
How can one goal fit both?

7
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:29.499
Picture the fixed rooms.

8
00:00:29.499 --> 00:00:31.623
Long-term storage is one big room.

9
00:00:31.623 --> 00:00:36.121
To clean any of it, the crew cleans all of it, while every worker waits.

10
00:00:36.121 --> 00:00:38.245
A bigger room means a longer wait.

11
00:00:39.133 --> 00:00:42.728
That's repainting the whole house because one wall has a scratch.

12
00:00:42.728 --> 00:00:47.595
What we'd like is to clean just the messiest corner, and leave the rest for another day.

13
00:00:48.467 --> 00:00:51.119
So G1's crew paints lines on the floor.

14
00:00:51.119 --> 00:00:58.396
It cuts the heap into equal bays, called regions, and hangs a card on each one that says its use right now.

15
00:00:58.396 --> 00:01:01.116
Eden, survivor, old, humongous, or free.

16
00:01:01.267 --> 00:01:02.227
Let's map it.

17
00:01:02.227 --> 00:01:03.851
The floor is the heap.

18
00:01:03.851 --> 00:01:05.401
Each bay is a region.

19
00:01:05.401 --> 00:01:08.281
The card is the region's role, for now.

20
00:01:08.281 --> 00:01:09.684
And the cards move.

21
00:01:09.867 --> 00:01:11.223
Watch a young pause.

22
00:01:11.223 --> 00:01:16.786
The crew copies the live boxes out of every eden and survivor bay, into free bays.

23
00:01:16.786 --> 00:01:19.499
The emptied bays get a fresh card: free.

24
00:01:19.499 --> 00:01:22.416
Next time, any of them can become anything.

25
00:01:23.333 --> 00:01:26.184
G1 is short for Garbage-First.

26
00:01:26.184 --> 00:01:33.501
On JDK 25 it's the default on most machines, and from JDK 27, on all of them.

27
00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:36.072
How big is one bay?

28
00:01:36.072 --> 00:01:45.400
G1 divides the max heap by 2048, keeps the answer between 1 and 32 MB, and rounds it up to a power of two.

29
00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:47.056
So, a 1 GB heap.

30
00:01:47.056 --> 00:01:49.241
How big are its regions?

31
00:01:49.241 --> 00:01:50.697
Pause and guess.

32
00:01:53.800 --> 00:01:54.245
1 MB.

33
00:01:54.245 --> 00:01:59.229
Half a megabyte is below the floor, so it's raised to 1.

34
00:01:59.229 --> 00:02:08.753
The 3.4 GB default heap on the lesson's machine got 2 MB regions, and a 40 GB heap gets the biggest, 32 MB.

35
00:02:09.667 --> 00:02:10.978
Now the sharp edge.

36
00:02:10.978 --> 00:02:14.843
An object larger than half a region is called humongous.

37
00:02:14.843 --> 00:02:21.676
It skips eden, and gets its own run of whole bays, side by side, which count as old from the start.

38
00:02:21.867 --> 00:02:27.013
Here's the lesson's photo: a 3 MiB byte array, with 1 MB regions.

39
00:02:27.013 --> 00:02:29.388
How many regions does it take?

40
00:02:29.388 --> 00:02:30.655
Pause and guess.

41
00:02:33.733 --> 00:02:34.099
Four.

42
00:02:34.099 --> 00:02:41.714
Every array carries a 16-byte header, so this one is 3 MiB plus 16 bytes, and that doesn't fit in three.

43
00:02:41.714 --> 00:02:44.569
Nearly all of the fourth bay is wasted.

44
00:02:45.467 --> 00:02:50.695
It's the suitcase that's one sock over the limit, so you pay for a second suitcase.

45
00:02:50.695 --> 00:02:53.404
Sixteen bytes just bought a whole megabyte.

46
00:02:53.404 --> 00:02:58.884
So the point is: an object just over a region boundary can waste almost a whole region.

47
00:02:59.800 --> 00:03:03.460
Each humongous object also needs free bays in a row.

48
00:03:03.460 --> 00:03:08.036
And a burst of them can start G1's background marking on its own.

49
00:03:08.036 --> 00:03:11.063
The log calls that G1 Humongous Allocation.

50
00:03:11.933 --> 00:03:16.569
The good news: G1 reclaims dead humongous objects early.

51
00:03:16.569 --> 00:03:23.853
In the lesson's demo on JDK 25, the first young pause logged humongous regions, 28 to 0.

52
00:03:23.853 --> 00:03:27.164
Seven dead photos, at four regions each.

53
00:03:28.067 --> 00:03:35.208
So if your log is full of G1 Humongous Allocation, stream those uploads instead of holding them whole.

54
00:03:35.208 --> 00:03:39.408
Or raise G1HeapRegionSize until they're under half a region.

55
00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:46.700
In Java, readAllBytes makes one array as big as the photo, and transferTo copies through a small buffer.

56
00:03:46.700 --> 00:03:49.704
In Kotlin, readBytes and copyTo do the same.

57
00:03:50.600 --> 00:03:52.700
Now, back to our question.

58
00:03:52.700 --> 00:03:57.305
G1's default goal, MaxGCPauseMillis, is 200 milliseconds.

59
00:03:57.305 --> 00:04:03.121
So G1 plans how much to clean in each pause, to stay inside that budget.

60
00:04:03.267 --> 00:04:05.378
Regions make the plan possible.

61
00:04:05.378 --> 00:04:12.599
G1 can clean part of the old generation without cleaning all of it, and it cleans the messiest bays first.

62
00:04:12.599 --> 00:04:15.937
Like tidying a garage with only an hour to spare.

63
00:04:16.133 --> 00:04:19.257
But first the crew must know how full each bay is.

64
00:04:19.257 --> 00:04:26.817
So from time to time, an inspector walks the long-term bays while work goes on, and notes how much of each is still live.

65
00:04:26.817 --> 00:04:28.441
That's concurrent marking.

66
00:04:28.600 --> 00:04:35.335
Then come mixed pauses: all the young bays, plus a few old ones, the ones with the most garbage.

67
00:04:35.335 --> 00:04:39.193
A bay more than 85 percent live isn't even a candidate.

68
00:04:39.193 --> 00:04:41.298
That choice gives G1 its name.

69
00:04:41.298 --> 00:04:42.280
Garbage-First.

70
00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:46.763
Garbage-First would be a terrible motto for a restaurant.

71
00:04:46.763 --> 00:04:48.888
For a cleaning crew, it's perfect.

72
00:04:48.888 --> 00:04:54.889
Copying costs what's live, so a mostly garbage bay gives back the most space for the least work.

73
00:04:55.800 --> 00:04:58.019
Here the warehouse picture breaks.

74
00:04:58.019 --> 00:05:01.935
A real crew could carry boxes while people work around them.

75
00:05:01.935 --> 00:05:07.679
G1 marks while the program runs, but it stops every thread before it moves a single box.

76
00:05:07.679 --> 00:05:09.245
That stop is your pause.

77
00:05:10.133 --> 00:05:11.111
So, the moral.

78
00:05:11.111 --> 00:05:20.749
G1 cuts the floor into equal bays, gives each one a card for now, and spends each pause's budget on the bays where cleaning pays the most.

79
00:05:21.667 --> 00:05:22.624
Quick check.

80
00:05:22.624 --> 00:05:23.899
Two old regions.

81
00:05:23.899 --> 00:05:27.488
One is 70 percent live, the other 20 percent.

82
00:05:27.488 --> 00:05:29.800
Which one does G1 take first?

83
00:05:33.067 --> 00:05:34.486
The 20 percent one.

84
00:05:34.486 --> 00:05:39.117
G1 copies a fifth of a region, and gets the whole region back.
